      "section_id": "sinus_forceps_section_1",
      "section_title": "Sinus forceps",
      "section_order": 1,
      "subsections": [
        {
          "subsection_id": "sinus_forceps_subsection_1",
          "subsection_title": "Overview",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "sinus_forceps_group_1",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/sinus_forceps_01.webp",
                  "caption": null
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "sinus_forceps_subsection_2",
          "subsection_title": "1. Introduction and identification",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "sinus_forceps_group_2",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Sinus forceps are long, slender surgical instruments used to explore, access or gently manipulate the opening of a sinus tract or a narrow wound, depending on the specific design."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "They may be used during selected procedures involving chronic discharging sinuses, abscess cavities and wound exploration. The precise function depends on the instrument pattern: some are primarily dissecting or spreading forceps, while others are designed for probing or drainage-related tasks."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "OSCE identification card"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Name: Sinus forceps"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Classification: Surgical exploration and dissecting instrument"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Primary function: Accessing and gently exploring suitable sinus openings or narrow wound cavities."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Characteristic feature: Long, slender working ends designed for access to confined spaces."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Mechanism: Depends on the model; some have ring handles and a ratchet, while others have a non-locking handle or probe-like design."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Important OSCE distinction: “Sinus forceps” is not a single universally standardized instrument design. If the instrument has long, narrow jaws that open to spread tissue, describe it as a sinus or sinus-dissecting forceps according to the label used in your institution. Do not confuse it with a simple sinus probe, which is generally a slender instrument used to trace a tract rather than grasp or spread tissue."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "sinus_forceps_subsection_3",
          "subsection_title": "2. Parts and labelling",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "sinus_forceps_group_3",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/sinus_forceps_02.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "slideshare.net"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The parts depend on the pattern presented, but for a ring-handled, locking sinus forceps, the following are commonly identifiable."
                },
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Part",
                    "Description and function"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Handles",
                      "Allow the operator to grip and control the instrument."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Ratchet lock",
                      "Maintains closure in models that have a locking mechanism."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Box joint or pivot",
                      "Allows the two halves to move relative to each other."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Shanks",
                      "Connect the handles to the working ends."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Long, slender jaws",
                      "Provide access to a narrow wound or cavity."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Jaw tips",
                      "May be blunt or tapered, depending on the design."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Gripping surfaces",
                      "Allow tissue to be held or manipulated in grasping models."
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "If the instrument is non-locking, do not invent a ratchet or box joint during the OSCE. Describe only the features actually present."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "sinus_forceps_subsection_4",
          "subsection_title": "3. Types and related instruments",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "sinus_forceps_group_4",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/sinus_forceps_03.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A. Sinus-dissecting forceps"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A long, narrow instrument used for controlled separation or exploration of suitable tissue planes in selected wound procedures."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/sinus_forceps_04.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "B. Sinus probe — related but different"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A slender, usually blunt-ended instrument used to assess the direction or course of an accessible sinus tract. It is not primarily a grasping forceps."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/sinus_forceps_05.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "C. Long artery forceps — common source of confusion"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A long haemostatic instrument that may be used in selected drainage procedures, but its primary function is clamping blood vessels or tissue, not tracing a sinus tract."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "These are related instruments, not interchangeable names for the same device. In an examination, prioritize the actual shape, mechanism and intended function of the instrument shown."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "sinus_forceps_subsection_5",
          "subsection_title": "4. Uses and clinical applications",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "sinus_forceps_group_5",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Depending on the design and clinical setting, sinus forceps may be used for:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Gentle exploration of an accessible sinus opening or narrow wound.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Separating selected superficial tissue planes during wound procedures.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Accessing a confined area when a suitable long, slender instrument is needed.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Assisting with selected procedures for chronic discharging sinuses.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Manipulating tissue or material within a wound when the specific instrument is designed for that purpose.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "sinus_forceps_group_6",
              "group_title": "Surgical procedures where they may be encountered",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Chronic sinus management: Selected procedures involving a discharging sinus.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Abscess drainage: Selected wound exploration or cavity procedures, where appropriate.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Wound exploration: Assessment or manipulation of accessible wound spaces.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "General surgery: Selected procedures involving superficial wound tracts.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Clinical caution: A sinus should not be blindly probed or forcefully explored. A tract may communicate with important structures, and aggressive probing can create a false passage, cause bleeding or injure deeper tissue. Assessment and treatment should be guided by the anatomy, clinical findings and appropriate imaging when indicated."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "sinus_forceps_subsection_6",
          "subsection_title": "5. Advantages",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "sinus_forceps_group_7",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Good access: The long, slender design can reach confined areas.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "1."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Controlled manipulation: Appropriate patterns permit controlled separation or grasping.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "2."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Improved visibility: A suitable long instrument can keep the operator's hands away from the immediate wound opening.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "3."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Useful in selected wound procedures: Can assist with access to superficial sinus openings and narrow operative spaces.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "4."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Specialized design: Certain patterns are more suitable for confined spaces than bulky general-purpose forceps.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "5."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "sinus_forceps_subsection_7",
          "subsection_title": "6. Disadvantages",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "sinus_forceps_group_8",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "The long, slender design may provide limited tactile feedback.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "1."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Narrow tips can injure tissue if used forcefully.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "2."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Blind exploration can lead to a false passage.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "3."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "It may be unsuitable for a deep or complex sinus without prior assessment of its course.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "4."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "The instrument is not necessarily suitable for grasping delicate tissue or controlling bleeding.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "5."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "The long jaws and joint require thorough cleaning and inspection.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "6."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "sinus_forceps_subsection_8",
          "subsection_title": "7. Complications of improper use",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "sinus_forceps_group_9",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Complication",
                    "Mechanism",
                    "Prevention"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Tissue trauma",
                      "Excessive force or inappropriate jaw placement",
                      "Use controlled, gentle manipulation."
                    ],
                    [
                      "False passage",
                      "Forcing an instrument into an uncertain tract",
                      "Avoid blind or forceful probing."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Bleeding",
                      "Injury to vascular tissue",
                      "Assess anatomy and avoid aggressive exploration."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Infection spread",
                      "Traumatic manipulation or poor aseptic technique",
                      "Use appropriate sterile technique and clinical judgment."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Injury to deeper structures",
                      "Unrecognized communication with deeper anatomy",
                      "Assess the tract and use imaging or specialist input when indicated."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Instrument damage",
                      "Excessive force or faulty joints",
                      "Inspect before use and avoid inappropriate leverage."
                    ]
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "sinus_forceps_subsection_9",
          "subsection_title": "8. Principles of safe handling",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "sinus_forceps_group_10",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/sinus_forceps_06.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Step 1 — Identify and inspect"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Confirm the instrument's actual design, check its tips and joints, and establish whether it is a grasping, dissecting or probing instrument."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/sinus_forceps_07.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Step 2 — Assess the wound"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Establish the indication and understand the likely anatomy before introducing an instrument into a tract or cavity."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/sinus_forceps_08.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Step 3 — Use controlled movements"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Avoid excessive force, blind deep exploration or using the instrument as a lever against vulnerable tissue."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Step 4 — Reprocess appropriately"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Clean the working end and joints thoroughly, inspect for damage and sterilize reusable instruments using a validated process."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "sinus_forceps_subsection_10",
          "subsection_title": "9. Sinus forceps versus sinus probe versus artery forceps",
